% SGA 1, Expose I, proof of Proposition I.10.1.
% French authority semantic boundary: lines 2044--2049,
% 6 LF lines / 367 bytes; LF-normalized SHA-256:
% BD95FBC222429DF8B699835A75B8C84A2D450E9C65334F51CE8B82673EE9BB46.
% The blank line 2050 closes the source envelope; Corollary I.10.2 begins
% at line 2051 and is excluded.
% Both checked print witnesses attest three source-oddity readings retained
% below: finite and dominant; normalization of X; and the field K_i of X.

By Corollary~\ref{I.9.10}, \(X\) is normal; a fortiori its local rings are
integral domains, so the connected components of~\(X\) are irreducible.
Since \(X_i\) is normal, finite, and dominant over~\(Y\), it follows from a
special case (an almost trivial one, moreover) of the Main Theorem that
\(X_i\) is an open subset of the normalization of~\(X\) in the field \(K_i\)
of~\(X\).

\paragraph{Editorial note on the proof.}
The French authority and both checked print witnesses read ``finite and
dominant,'' ``normalization of \(X\),'' and ``the field \(K_i\) of \(X\).''
In context, the Main Theorem argument appears to require ``quasi-finite and
dominant''; the normalization base appears to be \(Y\), as already noted
after the statement; and \(K_i\) was defined as the function field of
\(X_i\).  We retain all three attested readings and flag these tensions.
